  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_XT4rfZ58Z33Xs5pg",
   "name": "Basic Coordinate Geometry",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Basic Coordinate Geometry"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are Cartesian coordinates?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Cartesian** coordinates are the coordinates used in the *x-y *coordinate system\n\n  - They allow us to label where things are in a two-dimensional plane\n- In the 2D Cartesian system, the horizontal axis is labelled *x*  and the vertical axis is labelled *y*"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What can I do with coordinates?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If you have two points with coordinates $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$* *and* *$( x_{2} , y_{2} )$* *then you should be able to find\n\n  - The **distance** between the two points\n  - The point that **divides** the line between the two points in a given **ratio**\n  - The **gradient** of the line joining the two points"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the distance between two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **distance **$d$ between two points with coordinates $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$* *and* *$( x_{2} , y_{2} )$* *can be found using the formula\n\n  - $d = \\sqrt{( x_{1} - x_{2} )^{2} + ( y_{1} - y_{2} )^{2}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $d$ is the **hypotenuse** of a right-angled triangle\n\n  - The differences between the $x$-coordinates and the $y$-coordinates give the other two side lengths\n  - So by Pythagoras’ Theorem  $d^{2} = ( x_{1} - x_{2} )^{2} + ( y_{1} - y_{2} )^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide the line between two points in a given ratio?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The coordinates of the point **dividing** the line joining $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$ and $( x_{2} , y_{2} )$ in the **ratio** $m:n$ are given by\n\n  - $( \\frac{n x_{1} + m x_{2} }{m + n} , \\frac{n y_{1} + m y_{2} }{m + n} )$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If $m>n$ then the point is closer to $( x_{2} , y_{2} )$\n- If $m<n$ then the point is closer to $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the midpoint between two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **midpoint** is the point **halfway between** the two points\n\n  - This is just 'dividing in a given ratio' where the ratio is $1:1$\n- The **coordinates** of the **midpoint** will be\n\n  - $( \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2} }{2} , \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2} }{2} )$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/3.1.1-Basic-Coordinate-Geometry-Notes-Diagram-5.png",
   "file": "assets/0231e3ac2a05-3-1-1-basic-coordinate-geometry-notes-diagram-5.png",
   "alt": "Midpoint of a line segment",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the gradient of a straight line joining two points?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **gradient** $m$ of a line between two points with coordinates $( x_{1} , y_{1} )$* *and* *$( x_{2} , y_{2} )$* *can be found using the formula\n\n  - $m=\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This is also known as  $m=\\frac{\\mathrm{rise}}{\\mathrm{run}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Point $A$ has coordinates $( 3 , - 4 )$ and point $B$ has coordinates $( - 5 , 8 )$.\n\n(a) Calculate the distance between points $A$ and $B$.\n\n> *Let *$A ( 3 , - 4 )$*be 'point 1', so *$x_{1}=3$* and *$y_{1}=-4$\n\n*Let *$B ( - 5 , 8 )$ *be 'point 2', so *$x_{2}=-5$* and *$y_{2}=8$\n\n> *Use the formula  *$d = \\sqrt{( x_{1} - x_{2} )^{2} + ( y_{1} - y_{2} )^{2}}$\n\n$d = \\sqrt{( 3 - ( - 5 ) )^{2} + ( - 4 - 8 )^{2}} = \\sqrt{8^{2} + ( - 12 )^{2}} = \\sqrt{64 + 144}$\n\n**Final answer:** $\\sqrt{208}$**  ****(or **$4\\sqrt{13}$** ****or 14.4 to 3 s.f.)**\n\n(b) Find the gradient of the line joining points *A *and *B*.\n\n> *Let *$A ( 3 , - 4 )$* be 'point 1', so *$x_{1}=3$* and *$y_{1}=-4$\n\n*Let *$B ( - 5 , 8 )$ *be 'point 2', so *$x_{2}=-5$* and *$y_{2}=8$\n\n> *Use the formula *$m=\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{x_{2}-x_{1}}$\n\n$m = \\frac{8 - ( - 4 )}{- 5 - 3} = \\frac{12}{- 8}$\n\n> *Simplify the fraction*\n\n$-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\n(c) Find point that divides the line joining points *A* and *B* in the ratio $1:3$.\n\n> *Let *$A ( 3 , - 4 )$*be 'point 1', so *$x_{1}=3$* and *$y_{1}=-4$\n\n*Let *$B ( - 5 , 8 )$ *be 'point 2', so *$x_{2}=-5$* and *$y_{2}=8$\n\n> *Use the formula  *$( \\frac{n x_{1} + m x_{2} }{m + n} , \\frac{n y_{1} + m y_{2} }{m + n} )$* with *$m=1$* and *$n=3$\n\n> $( \\frac{( 3 ( 3 ) + 1 ( - 5 ) )}{1 + 3} , \\frac{( 3 ( - 4 ) + 1 ( 8 ) )}{1 + 3} ) = ( \\frac{9 - 5}{4} , \\frac{- 12 + 8}{4} )$* *\n\n$(1,-1)$"
